/* ---------- LEARN: concepts by module ---------- */
/* p = key points, say = one-line answer to say out loud, trap = common mistake */
const LEARN = [
{id:"arch", t:"Network architecture", s:"Who talks to whom, and over which interface.", lessons:[
 {id:"a1", t:"The big picture: UE, RAN, core",
  p:["A mobile network has three parts: the <b>UE</b> (phone), the <b>RAN</b> (base stations), and the <b>core</b> (subscriber, session and routing functions).",
     "<b>LTE:</b> the base station is the <b>eNB</b>. The core is the <b>EPC</b>: MME (signaling, mobility), SGW and PGW (user data), HSS (subscriber database).",
     "<b>5G NR:</b> the base station is the <b>gNB</b>. The core is the <b>5GC</b>: AMF (access and mobility), SMF (sessions), UPF (user data), UDM and AUSF (subscriber data, authentication).",
     "LTE interfaces: <code>Uu</code> (UE to eNB, the air interface), <code>S1-MME</code> (eNB to MME, protocol S1AP), <code>S1-U</code> (eNB to SGW, GTP-U), <code>X2</code> (eNB to eNB).",
     "NR interfaces: <code>Uu</code>, <code>N2</code> (gNB to AMF, protocol NGAP), <code>N3</code> (gNB to UPF, GTP-U), <code>Xn</code> (gNB to gNB). N2 and N3 together are called NG.",
     "This role tests the RAN: the eNB/gNB software stack that speaks PHY, MAC, RLC, PDCP and RRC to the phone, and S1AP/NGAP to the core."],
  say:"The eNB or gNB is the radio side of the network. It runs PHY up to RRC towards the phone and S1 or NG towards the core.",
  trap:"Do not mix up the names. MME, SGW, PGW belong to LTE. AMF, SMF, UPF belong to 5G."},
 {id:"a2", t:"The protocol stack, layer by layer",
  p:["<b>NAS</b> (UE to core): registration, authentication, session setup. The base station only relays it.",
     "<b>RRC</b> (UE to eNB/gNB): system information, connection setup, radio bearers, measurements, handover.",
     "<b>SDAP</b> (NR only, user plane): maps QoS flows to data radio bearers.",
     "<b>PDCP</b>: ciphering, integrity protection, header compression (ROHC), reordering.",
     "<b>RLC</b>: segmentation and, in acknowledged mode (AM), retransmission (ARQ).",
     "<b>MAC</b>: scheduling, HARQ, random access, multiplexing of logical channels.",
     "<b>PHY</b>: coding, modulation, OFDM, reference signals, measurements.",
     "Control plane stack: NAS / RRC / PDCP / RLC / MAC / PHY. User plane stack: IP / (SDAP) / PDCP / RLC / MAC / PHY."],
  say:"NAS talks to the core, RRC controls the radio link, PDCP secures, RLC makes it reliable, MAC schedules and does HARQ, PHY puts bits on the air.",
  trap:"There are two retransmission layers. HARQ in MAC is fast. ARQ in RLC is slower and catches what HARQ missed."},
 {id:"a3", t:"NSA and SA, bearers",
  p:["<b>NSA (EN-DC, option 3x):</b> the phone is anchored on LTE (master node, MCG) and NR is added as a secondary node (SCG). The core is still the EPC.",
     "<b>SA (option 2):</b> NR only, with the 5G core. Registration instead of attach, PDU session instead of PDN connection.",
     "<b>SRB</b> = signaling radio bearer. SRB0 carries the first RRC messages on the common channel. SRB1 carries RRC (and early NAS). SRB2 carries NAS after security is on. SRB3 is the direct NR SRB in EN-DC.",
     "<b>DRB</b> = data radio bearer. LTE: one EPS bearer maps to one DRB. NR: QoS flows map to DRBs through SDAP.",
     "QoS class: QCI in LTE (for example QCI 9 default internet, QCI 1 voice, QCI 5 IMS signaling). 5QI in 5G."],
  say:"NSA adds an NR leg to an LTE anchor on the EPC. SA is NR with the 5G core end to end.",
  trap:"In NSA the NAS is LTE NAS. You will not see a 5G Registration Request in an NSA log."},
 {id:"a4", t:"Inside a gNB, and where it sits on a satellite",
  p:["A gNB can be split into <b>CU</b> (RRC, PDCP), <b>DU</b> (RLC, MAC, upper PHY) and <b>RU</b> (lower PHY, RF). CU to DU is the <code>F1</code> interface.",
     "For satellites there are two designs. <b>Transparent</b> (bent pipe): the satellite only repeats the signal and the base station is on the ground. <b>Regenerative</b>: the base station, or part of it, flies on the satellite.",
     "3GPP Release 17 NTN covers the transparent design. Regenerative payloads are being added in later releases.",
     "SpaceX has said publicly that each direct-to-cell satellite carries an LTE modem that works like a cell tower in space, with laser links back to the Starlink network. That means base station functions are on the satellite.",
     "Why it matters for testing: with the base station on the satellite, the delay between phone and base station is only the service link (a few milliseconds), not service link plus feeder link."],
  say:"Transparent means the satellite is a repeater. Regenerative means the base station is on board. Starlink direct-to-cell describes itself as a cell tower in space.",
  trap:"Do not claim to know SpaceX internal design. Say what is public, then reason from physics."},
 {id:"a5", t:"Channels: logical, transport, physical",
  p:["<b>Logical channels</b> say what kind of information: BCCH (broadcast), PCCH (paging), CCCH (common control), DCCH (dedicated control), DTCH (dedicated traffic).",
     "<b>Transport channels</b> say how it is carried: BCH, PCH, DL-SCH, UL-SCH, RACH.",
     "<b>Physical channels</b> are what is on the air. Downlink: PBCH (MIB), PDCCH (control, carries DCI), PDSCH (data, SIBs, paging). Uplink: PRACH (preamble), PUCCH (control, carries UCI), PUSCH (data).",
     "<b>DCI</b> = downlink control information: grants and assignments. <b>UCI</b> = uplink control information: HARQ ACK/NACK, scheduling request, channel state (CQI/PMI/RI).",
     "LTE also has PCFICH and PHICH. NR removed both."],
  say:"PDCCH tells the phone where its data is. PDSCH and PUSCH carry the data. PUCCH carries ACKs, scheduling requests and channel reports.",
  trap:"SIBs are sent on PDSCH, not on PBCH. Only the MIB is on PBCH."},
 {id:"a6", t:"Identifiers you will see in logs",
  p:["<b>Subscriber:</b> IMSI (LTE), SUPI and its concealed form SUCI (5G). Temporary: GUTI, 5G-GUTI, S-TMSI.",
     "<b>Radio (RNTI):</b> C-RNTI (your connection), RA-RNTI (random access response), TC-RNTI (temporary, during random access), P-RNTI (paging), SI-RNTI (system information).",
     "<b>Cell:</b> PCI (physical cell ID: 504 values in LTE, 1008 in NR), global cell ID (ECGI, NCGI), TAC (tracking area code), PLMN (MCC + MNC).",
     "<b>Frequency:</b> EARFCN (LTE channel number), NR-ARFCN, GSCN (NR sync raster), band number (for example band 25, n25, n77).",
     "<b>Bearer:</b> EPS bearer ID, DRB ID, LCID (logical channel ID), QFI (QoS flow ID)."],
  say:"When I open a log, I first pin down PLMN, band, channel number, PCI and the RNTI, so I know exactly which cell and which connection I am reading.",
  trap:"PCI is not unique. Two cells can share a PCI (PCI confusion or collision), which breaks handover."}
]},

{id:"phy", t:"PHY layer", s:"OFDM, sync, control and data channels, measurements, link adaptation.", lessons:[
 {id:"b1", t:"OFDM grid and numerology",
  p:["LTE: subcarrier spacing (SCS) is fixed at <b>15 kHz</b>. One subframe is 1 ms. One slot has 7 symbols, so 14 symbols per subframe (normal CP).",
     "One <b>resource block (RB)</b> is 12 subcarriers. In LTE that is 180 kHz. LTE bandwidths: 1.4, 3, 5, 10, 15, 20 MHz give 6, 15, 25, 50, 75, 100 RBs.",
     "NR: SCS = 15 x 2^&mu; kHz. &mu;=0..3 gives 15, 30, 60, 120 kHz. A slot is always 14 symbols, so a higher SCS means a shorter slot (1 ms, 0.5 ms, 0.25 ms, 0.125 ms).",
     "<b>FR1</b> is 410 MHz to 7.125 GHz and uses 15, 30 or 60 kHz, up to 100 MHz bandwidth. <b>FR2</b> is mmWave (24.25 GHz and up) and uses 60 or 120 kHz, up to 400 MHz.",
     "The <b>cyclic prefix (CP)</b> is a guard at the start of each symbol. It absorbs delay spread and small timing errors. LTE normal CP is about 4.7 &micro;s.",
     "A <b>resource element (RE)</b> is one subcarrier in one symbol. It is the smallest unit and carries one modulation symbol."],
  say:"LTE is locked to 15 kHz. NR scales the subcarrier spacing, which trades symbol length against tolerance to Doppler and phase noise.",
  trap:"Wider SCS tolerates more Doppler but has a shorter CP, so it tolerates less timing error. Both matter on a satellite link."},
 {id:"b2", t:"FDD, TDD and TDD patterns",
  p:["<b>FDD:</b> uplink and downlink on separate paired frequencies, at the same time.",
     "<b>TDD:</b> one frequency, uplink and downlink take turns. A guard period sits at each downlink-to-uplink switch.",
     "LTE TDD has 7 fixed uplink-downlink configurations (0 to 6) and a special subframe (DwPTS, GP, UpPTS).",
     "NR TDD is set by <code>tdd-UL-DL-ConfigurationCommon</code>: a period, a number of downlink slots and symbols, and a number of uplink slots and symbols. A common pattern is DDDSU.",
     "Tuning trade-off: more downlink slots give more downlink throughput but less uplink capacity and slower uplink feedback.",
     "The guard period must cover the round-trip propagation time. Larger cell or longer path needs a longer guard."],
  say:"The TDD pattern sets the downlink to uplink split. I pick it from the traffic mix and check the guard period fits the cell range.",
  trap:"Two TDD cells with different patterns interfere with each other (cross-link interference). Patterns must be aligned."},
 {id:"b3", t:"Cell search and synchronization",
  p:["The phone first finds <b>PSS</b> and <b>SSS</b> (primary and secondary sync signals). Together they give timing, frequency offset and the PCI.",
     "LTE: PSS/SSS repeat every 5 ms. The <b>MIB</b> is on PBCH with a 40 ms period and gives bandwidth, PHICH config and frame number.",
     "NR: PSS, SSS and PBCH are packed in an <b>SSB</b> block (4 symbols, 20 RBs). SSBs are sent in a burst, one per beam. The phone assumes a 20 ms period for initial search.",
     "The NR MIB points to <b>CORESET 0</b> and search space 0, where the phone finds the DCI for <b>SIB1</b>.",
     "After MIB and SIB1 the phone knows PLMN, tracking area, whether the cell is barred, minimum signal level, and the random access configuration.",
     "A phone can only lock if the frequency error is within what its search can handle. A large uncorrected Doppler shift can make the cell invisible."],
  say:"PSS and SSS give timing and cell ID, the MIB tells the phone where to find SIB1, and SIB1 tells it how to access the cell.",
  trap:"If a phone never camps, check sync and SIB1 decoding before you look at RACH. No SIB1 means no RACH attempt at all."},
 {id:"b4", t:"Downlink control: PDCCH and DCI",
  p:["Every scheduled transmission starts with a <b>DCI</b> on PDCCH. The DCI's CRC is scrambled with an RNTI, so only the target phone accepts it.",
     "LTE DCI formats: 0 (uplink grant), 1A/1/2/2A (downlink assignments). NR: 0_0 and 0_1 (uplink), 1_0 and 1_1 (downlink).",
     "NR places PDCCH in a <b>CORESET</b> (the time-frequency area) and a <b>search space</b> (when and where the phone looks).",
     "<b>Aggregation level</b> (1, 2, 4, 8, 16 CCEs) is PDCCH's link adaptation. Weak phones need a higher level.",
     "The phone does <b>blind decoding</b>: it tries candidates and keeps the one whose CRC matches.",
     "If the phone misses the DCI, it sends nothing back. The network sees <b>DTX</b>, not a NACK."],
  say:"The DCI is the grant. If PDCCH fails, the data never has a chance, and the sign is DTX instead of NACK.",
  trap:"High BLER and many DTX are different problems. NACK means PDSCH failed. DTX points to PDCCH or uplink feedback."},
 {id:"b5", t:"Data channels, coding and reference signals",
  p:["<b>PDSCH</b> and <b>PUSCH</b> carry data. Modulation is QPSK, 16QAM, 64QAM or 256QAM (2, 4, 6, 8 bits per symbol).",
     "Coding: LTE uses turbo codes for data. NR uses <b>LDPC</b> for data and <b>polar</b> codes for control.",
     "LTE has <b>CRS</b> (cell-specific reference signal), always on, used for measurement and demodulation.",
     "NR has no always-on CRS. It uses <b>DMRS</b> (demodulation, sent with the data), <b>CSI-RS</b> (channel measurement), <b>TRS</b> (tracking), <b>PT-RS</b> (phase tracking, mainly FR2) and <b>SRS</b> (uplink sounding).",
     "The transport block size (TBS) comes from MCS, number of RBs and number of layers."],
  say:"LTE measures on always-on CRS. NR measures on SSB and CSI-RS and demodulates with DMRS that travels with the data.",
  trap:"RSRP in LTE is measured on CRS. In NR it is SS-RSRP or CSI-RSRP. Say which one when you quote a number."},
 {id:"b6", t:"Link adaptation: CQI, MCS, BLER",
  p:["The phone measures the downlink and reports <b>CQI</b> (0 to 15), <b>RI</b> (how many layers) and <b>PMI</b> (which precoder).",
     "The scheduler picks an <b>MCS</b> so that the first transmission fails about <b>10%</b> of the time. That is the usual BLER target. HARQ recovers the rest.",
     "<b>Outer-loop link adaptation (OLLA)</b> nudges the MCS up after ACKs and down after NACKs to hold the target.",
     "Uplink: the base station measures SINR on DMRS or SRS and picks the uplink MCS itself.",
     "Good SINR but low MCS or high BLER? Suspect wrong CQI reports, interference bursts, frequency or timing offset, RF path problems, or PA compression.",
     "BLER near 0% is not a win. It usually means the MCS is too conservative and throughput is left on the table."],
  say:"CQI goes up, MCS comes down as a decision, and the outer loop holds BLER near ten percent.",
  trap:"Initial BLER and residual BLER are different. Residual BLER (after all HARQ retransmissions) should be close to zero."},
 {id:"b7", t:"Measurements: RSRP, RSRQ, SINR",
  p:["<b>RSRP</b>: average power of one reference signal resource element, in dBm. It is signal strength.",
     "<b>RSSI</b>: total received power in the measurement bandwidth, including interference and noise.",
     "<b>RSRQ</b> = N x RSRP / RSSI (N = number of RBs). It is a quality measure that drops as load and interference rise.",
     "<b>SINR</b>: signal over interference plus noise. It is the best predictor of throughput.",
     "Rule-of-thumb RSRP: about -80 dBm strong, -100 fair, -110 weak, around -120 cell edge.",
     "Rule-of-thumb SINR: above 20 dB excellent, 13 to 20 good, 0 to 13 usable, below 0 poor.",
     "Good RSRP with bad SINR means interference. Bad RSRP with good SINR means a clean but weak link (noise-limited)."],
  say:"RSRP tells me coverage, SINR tells me quality. I always read them together.",
  trap:"RSRP is per resource element, so it is much lower than total channel power. For a full 5 MHz carrier the gap is about 25 dB."},
 {id:"b8", t:"Uplink power control and timing advance",
  p:["Uplink power: <code>P = min(Pcmax, P0 + alpha x PL + 10log10(M) + delta + f)</code>. P0 is the target per RB, alpha is how much path loss is compensated, M is RBs, f is closed-loop TPC commands.",
     "Typical phone maximum is <b>23 dBm</b> (power class 3), which is 0.2 W.",
     "<b>PHR</b> (power headroom report) tells the base station how much power the phone has left. Zero or negative headroom means the phone is power-limited.",
     "<b>Timing advance (TA)</b>: the phone sends early so its uplink arrives aligned with other phones. The first TA comes in the random access response. Later updates come as MAC control elements.",
     "LTE TA step is 16 Ts = 0.52 &micro;s, about 78 m of distance. The maximum initial TA of 1282 covers about 100 km of cell radius.",
     "If <code>timeAlignmentTimer</code> expires, the phone is out of uplink sync and must do random access again."],
  say:"Power control keeps the received uplink level right. Timing advance keeps uplink arrival times aligned inside the cyclic prefix.",
  trap:"The standard LTE timing advance range stops near 100 km. A satellite is hundreds of kilometers away. That is one of the core direct-to-cell problems."},
 {id:"b9", t:"MIMO and beams",
  p:["<b>Layers</b> are parallel data streams. Rank 2 means two streams. The phone reports the rank it can support (RI).",
     "LTE transmission modes: TM1 single antenna, TM2 transmit diversity, TM3 open-loop spatial multiplexing, TM4 closed-loop.",
     "NR uses beams. Each SSB is sent on a beam. The phone picks the best SSB, and that choice sets which PRACH occasion it uses.",
     "Beam management: beam sweeping, measurement, reporting, and <b>beam failure recovery</b> when the serving beam is lost.",
     "A satellite phased array forms many spot beams on the ground. Each beam is a cell or part of a cell."],
  say:"Rank is the number of streams. Beams focus energy, and on a satellite the beams are what make the link budget close.",
  trap:"With one phone antenna in bad signal you get rank 1. Do not expect MIMO gains at the edge of coverage."},
 {id:"b10", t:"Throughput math you can do on a whiteboard",
  p:["Peak rate is roughly: REs per second x bits per symbol x code rate x layers, minus overhead (about 25%).",
     "LTE 20 MHz: 100 RBs x 12 subcarriers x 14 symbols x 1000 per second = 16.8 million REs per second.",
     "At 64QAM (6 bits), code rate near 0.9, minus overhead, one layer gives about 75 Mbps. Two layers give about 150 Mbps. That is the well-known Cat 4 number.",
     "5 MHz is one quarter of that: about 18 Mbps per layer at the very top MCS.",
     "At low SINR you run QPSK with a low code rate. Then 5 MHz gives only 1 to 3 Mbps for the whole cell, shared by every user in the beam.",
     "Uplink is worse: the phone has 0.2 W and often gets only a few RBs so it can concentrate power."],
  say:"I estimate throughput from RBs, modulation, code rate and layers, then compare with the measured number to see how far off we are and why.",
  trap:"Cell throughput is shared. A satellite beam covers a wide area, so per-user rate depends heavily on how many users are in it."}
]},

{id:"mac", t:"MAC layer", s:"Random access, scheduling, HARQ, DRX.", lessons:[
 {id:"c1", t:"Random access (RACH)",
  p:["Used for: first access, re-establishment, handover, uplink data when out of sync, scheduling request failure, beam failure recovery.",
     "<b>Msg1:</b> the phone sends a <b>preamble</b> on PRACH. There are 64 preambles per cell, built from Zadoff-Chu sequences.",
     "<b>Msg2:</b> the base station answers with a <b>random access response (RAR)</b>, addressed to RA-RNTI, inside <code>ra-ResponseWindow</code>. It carries the timing advance, an uplink grant and a temporary C-RNTI.",
     "<b>Msg3:</b> the phone sends its identity on PUSCH (for example RRC Connection Request or RRCSetupRequest).",
     "<b>Msg4:</b> contention resolution. The phone must see its own identity echoed before <code>mac-ContentionResolutionTimer</code> expires.",
     "<b>Contention-based</b> (CBRA): phone picks a random preamble. <b>Contention-free</b> (CFRA): network assigns one, used in handover.",
     "<b>Power ramping:</b> if no RAR arrives, the phone raises power by <code>powerRampingStep</code> and tries again, up to <code>preambleTransMax</code>.",
     "NR Release 16 adds <b>2-step RACH</b>: MsgA (preamble + payload) and MsgB."],
  say:"Preamble, response with timing advance and grant, identity on Msg3, contention resolution on Msg4.",
  trap:"The preamble format and <code>zeroCorrelationZoneConfig</code> set the largest cell radius the base station can detect. Too small and far phones are never heard."},
 {id:"c2", t:"Scheduling and grants",
  p:["The scheduler in the base station decides, every slot, who gets which RBs and which MCS.",
     "Downlink: DCI on PDCCH, then data on PDSCH. Uplink: DCI grant, then data on PUSCH.",
     "LTE FDD timing is fixed: uplink data 4 ms after the grant, HARQ feedback 4 ms after the data.",
     "NR timing is flexible: <code>K0</code> (DCI to PDSCH), <code>K1</code> (PDSCH to HARQ-ACK), <code>K2</code> (DCI to PUSCH).",
     "Uplink flow: phone sends a <b>scheduling request (SR)</b> on PUCCH, gets a small grant, sends a <b>buffer status report (BSR)</b>, gets a bigger grant.",
     "Scheduler policies: round robin (fair), max C/I (best throughput), proportional fair (balance). Semi-persistent or configured grants avoid a DCI for every packet (voice)."],
  say:"Downlink is assign-then-send. Uplink is request, grant, report buffer, bigger grant.",
  trap:"Slow uplink start is often an SR problem: long SR period, SR not detected, or a prohibit timer."},
 {id:"c3", t:"HARQ",
  p:["<b>HARQ</b> = fast retransmission in MAC with soft combining. The receiver keeps the failed attempt and combines it with the retransmission.",
     "It runs as several parallel stop-and-wait <b>processes</b>. LTE FDD has 8 in the downlink. NR allows up to 16 (and up to 32 for NTN in Release 17).",
     "<b>NDI</b> (new data indicator) toggles for new data and stays the same for a retransmission.",
     "<b>Redundancy versions</b> (RV 0, 2, 3, 1) send different parity bits each time (incremental redundancy).",
     "LTE uplink HARQ is synchronous. LTE downlink and all NR HARQ are asynchronous (the DCI names the process).",
     "If HARQ gives up after the maximum retransmissions, RLC AM retransmits the data.",
     "HARQ assumes a short, known round trip. On a long-delay link the processes run out (HARQ stalling) unless you add processes or switch feedback off."],
  say:"HARQ is the fast repair loop at MAC with soft combining. RLC ARQ is the slower safety net above it.",
  trap:"BLER is counted on first transmissions. A link with 10% BLER and working HARQ loses almost nothing."},
 {id:"c4", t:"BSR, PHR, MAC control elements, DRX",
  p:["<b>MAC CEs</b> are small control messages inside MAC: timing advance command, BSR, PHR, DRX command, contention resolution identity, SCell activation.",
     "<b>BSR</b> reports how much uplink data waits, per logical channel group.",
     "<b>PHR</b> reports spare transmit power.",
     "<b>DRX</b> lets the phone sleep. Key timers: <code>onDurationTimer</code>, <code>drx-InactivityTimer</code>, long and short cycle, retransmission timer.",
     "Connected-mode DRX saves battery but adds latency: the network must wait for the next on-duration.",
     "Idle-mode DRX is the paging cycle. The phone wakes only at its paging occasion."],
  say:"DRX trades latency for battery. BSR and PHR are how the phone tells the scheduler what it needs and what it can afford.",
  trap:"A test that fails only sometimes with long latency may just be hitting DRX sleep. Check the DRX config before you file a bug."},
 {id:"c5", t:"MAC-level signs of trouble",
  p:["<b>RACH:</b> many Msg1 with no Msg2 (preamble not heard, or RAR not decoded). Msg3 sent but no Msg4 (contention or uplink quality).",
     "<b>Downlink:</b> high first-transmission BLER, many DTX, MCS stuck low, rank stuck at 1.",
     "<b>Uplink:</b> BSR keeps growing (not enough grants), PHR at or below zero (power-limited), many uplink retransmissions.",
     "<b>Sync:</b> timing advance drifting quickly, time alignment timer expiry, then RACH again.",
     "<b>Scheduling:</b> SR sent repeatedly without a grant, until <code>sr-TransMax</code> forces a RACH."],
  say:"I read MAC stats as symptoms: BLER and DTX for downlink, BSR and PHR for uplink, RACH counters for access.",
  trap:""}
]},

{id:"l2", t:"RLC and PDCP", s:"Reliability, ordering and security above MAC.", lessons:[
 {id:"d1", t:"RLC modes and ARQ",
  p:["<b>TM</b> (transparent): no header. Used for broadcast, paging, SRB0.",
     "<b>UM</b> (unacknowledged): segmentation, no retransmission. Used for voice and other delay-sensitive traffic.",
     "<b>AM</b> (acknowledged): segmentation plus retransmission. Used for SRBs and most data bearers.",
     "AM works with <b>status reports</b> (ACK and NACK of sequence numbers). The sender asks for one with a <b>poll</b> bit.",
     "Key timers: <code>t-PollRetransmit</code>, <code>t-StatusProhibit</code>, and <code>t-Reordering</code> (LTE) or <code>t-Reassembly</code> (NR).",
     "If one PDU is retransmitted <code>maxRetxThreshold</code> times without success, RLC reports to RRC and that triggers <b>radio link failure</b>."],
  say:"RLC AM is the reliable mode. If it hits the maximum retransmissions, the connection is declared failed.",
  trap:"The reordering timer must be longer than the worst HARQ delay. If it is too short, RLC asks for retransmissions that were still on their way."},
 {id:"d2", t:"PDCP and SDAP",
  p:["PDCP does <b>ciphering</b> for all bearers and <b>integrity protection</b> for signaling (and optionally for data in NR).",
     "It does <b>header compression</b> (ROHC), which matters most for voice.",
     "It reorders and removes duplicates, which matters at handover and with split bearers.",
     "A <b>discard timer</b> drops packets that waited too long in the buffer.",
     "NR adds <b>SDAP</b> above PDCP to map QoS flows to DRBs.",
     "Security starts with the <b>Security Mode Command</b> at RRC. A cipher or integrity mismatch shows up as integrity check failures or garbage after decryption."],
  say:"PDCP is security, compression and ordering. In NR, SDAP sits on top to map QoS flows to bearers.",
  trap:"After handover, data stalls are often PDCP sequence number or key (KeNB/KgNB) problems, not radio problems."},
 {id:"d3", t:"How layer 2 hurts throughput",
  p:["RLC retransmissions add delay. TCP sees delay and jitter and slows down.",
     "Out-of-order delivery makes TCP send duplicate ACKs and cut its window.",
     "A stalled RLC window (waiting for one missing PDU) blocks everything behind it.",
     "UDP iperf shows what the radio can do. TCP iperf shows what the user gets. Run both.",
     "If UDP is fine and TCP is poor, look at latency, jitter, reordering, MTU and window sizes before blaming the radio."],
  say:"I separate radio capacity from transport behavior by testing UDP and TCP side by side.",
  trap:""}
]},

{id:"rrc", t:"RRC and NAS", s:"States, system information, connection setup, mobility, failures.", lessons:[
 {id:"e1", t:"RRC states",
  p:["<b>LTE:</b> RRC_IDLE and RRC_CONNECTED.",
     "<b>NR:</b> RRC_IDLE, RRC_CONNECTED and <b>RRC_INACTIVE</b>. In inactive, the context is kept so the phone can resume quickly.",
     "Idle: the phone picks cells by itself (reselection), listens for paging, and has no radio bearers.",
     "Connected: the network controls mobility (handover), and the phone reports measurements.",
     "An <b>inactivity timer</b> in the base station releases idle connections. Short timer saves resources but causes more signaling."],
  say:"Idle means the phone manages itself. Connected means the network manages it. NR adds inactive as a fast-resume middle state.",
  trap:""},
 {id:"e2", t:"System information",
  p:["<b>MIB:</b> the minimum to find SIB1.",
     "<b>SIB1:</b> PLMN list, tracking area code, cell identity, barring, minimum signal level (<code>q-RxLevMin</code>), and the schedule of other SIBs. In NR it also carries the common serving cell config, including RACH.",
     "<b>LTE SIB2:</b> common radio config: RACH, PRACH, PUCCH, PUSCH, power control, timers (T300, T310 and so on).",
     "<b>SIB3 to SIB5:</b> cell reselection rules and neighbors.",
     "<b>NTN:</b> NR SIB19 carries satellite ephemeris, common timing advance and timing offsets. LTE IoT-NTN uses SIB31.",
     "When system information changes, phones are told through paging or a value tag."],
  say:"SIB1 answers 'may I camp here and how do I access'. A lot of RAN parameter tuning is really SIB content.",
  trap:""},
 {id:"e3", t:"RRC connection setup",
  p:["LTE: <code>RRCConnectionRequest</code> (Msg3), <code>RRCConnectionSetup</code> (Msg4), <code>RRCConnectionSetupComplete</code> (carries the first NAS message).",
     "NR: <code>RRCSetupRequest</code>, <code>RRCSetup</code>, <code>RRCSetupComplete</code>.",
     "Then: NAS authentication, NAS security, <code>SecurityModeCommand</code> / <code>SecurityModeComplete</code> at RRC, <code>UECapabilityEnquiry</code> / <code>UECapabilityInformation</code>.",
     "Then <code>RRCConnectionReconfiguration</code> (LTE) or <code>RRCReconfiguration</code> (NR) sets up SRB2, data bearers and measurement config.",
     "The establishment cause (mo-Signalling, mo-Data, mt-Access, emergency) tells the network why the phone is connecting.",
     "<code>RRCConnectionReject</code> / <code>RRCReject</code> with a wait time means the cell is overloaded or admission failed."],
  say:"Request, setup, setup complete with NAS inside, security, capabilities, then reconfiguration to add bearers.",
  trap:"T300 guards the setup. If it expires, the phone never got Msg4. That is a radio or RACH problem, not a NAS problem."},
 {id:"e4", t:"NAS: attach and registration",
  p:["<b>LTE attach:</b> Attach Request (with PDN Connectivity Request), Authentication Request/Response, Security Mode Command/Complete, Attach Accept (with Activate Default EPS Bearer Context Request), Attach Complete.",
     "<b>5G SA:</b> Registration Request, Authentication, Security Mode, Registration Accept, Registration Complete, then PDU Session Establishment Request/Accept.",
     "Other LTE procedures: Tracking Area Update, Service Request (idle to connected), Detach. 5G equivalents: mobility registration update, Service Request, Deregistration.",
     "Authentication is <b>AKA</b>: the network sends RAND and AUTN, the SIM checks AUTN and answers with RES.",
     "A satellite network that works as a roaming partner adds PLMN selection to the picture: the phone must decide to use the satellite PLMN only when it has no terrestrial coverage."],
  say:"Attach is: request, authenticate, secure, accept with a default bearer, complete. 5G splits registration and session setup into two procedures.",
  trap:"A reject is not a crash. Always read the cause value first."},
 {id:"e5", t:"Reject causes worth knowing",
  p:["<b>#3</b> Illegal UE, <b>#6</b> Illegal ME: authentication or equipment identity failed.",
     "<b>#7</b> EPS (or 5GS) services not allowed: subscription has no LTE or 5G service.",
     "<b>#11</b> PLMN not allowed, <b>#12</b> Tracking area not allowed, <b>#13</b> Roaming not allowed in this tracking area, <b>#15</b> No suitable cells in tracking area.",
     "<b>#17</b> Network failure, <b>#19</b> ESM failure (look inside for the ESM cause), <b>#22</b> Congestion (with a back-off timer).",
     "ESM / 5GSM: <b>#27</b> Missing or unknown APN/DNN, <b>#33</b> Requested service option not subscribed.",
     "5G only: <b>#27</b> (5GMM) N1 mode not allowed, <b>#62</b> No network slices available.",
     "Some causes put the PLMN or tracking area on a forbidden list, so the phone will not retry until reboot or timer. A test must clear that state."],
  say:"I read the cause, then ask: is it subscription, roaming rules, network failure, or congestion?",
  trap:"After cause #11 the phone may refuse to try that PLMN again. A later 'failure' may be the phone obeying the forbidden list."},
 {id:"e6", t:"Timers and counters",
  p:["<b>T300:</b> RRC connection setup. <b>T301:</b> re-establishment request. <b>T304:</b> handover. <b>T311:</b> cell search after failure.",
     "<b>N310 / T310 / N311:</b> after N310 out-of-sync indications, T310 starts. N311 in-sync indications stop it. If T310 expires: radio link failure.",
     "<b>T3410</b> (LTE attach, 15 s), <b>T3411</b> (retry after 10 s), <b>T3402</b> (12 minutes after 5 failed attempts), <b>T3412</b> (periodic tracking area update).",
     "5G: <b>T3510</b> (registration, 15 s), <b>T3511</b> (10 s), <b>T3502</b> (12 minutes), <b>T3512</b> (periodic registration).",
     "Each timer is a trade-off. Short T310 drops calls on brief fades. Long T310 keeps the phone stuck on a dead link."],
  say:"T310 with N310 and N311 decides when a bad link becomes a failure. T300 and T304 guard setup and handover.",
  trap:"If a test shows a retry every 10 seconds five times and then silence for 12 minutes, that is T3411 and T3402 working as designed."},
 {id:"e7", t:"Measurement events",
  p:["<b>A1:</b> serving better than threshold. <b>A2:</b> serving worse than threshold.",
     "<b>A3:</b> neighbor better than serving by an offset. The usual handover trigger.",
     "<b>A4:</b> neighbor better than threshold. <b>A5:</b> serving worse than threshold 1 and neighbor better than threshold 2.",
     "<b>B1 / B2:</b> the same ideas for a neighbor on another radio technology.",
     "Each event has <b>hysteresis</b> and <b>time-to-trigger (TTT)</b>. Results are smoothed by a layer 3 filter coefficient.",
     "Too easy to trigger: early handovers and ping-pong. Too hard: late handovers and drops."],
  say:"A3 is 'neighbor beats serving by an offset for long enough'. Offset, hysteresis and time-to-trigger are the three knobs.",
  trap:"On a moving-satellite cell, signal strength hardly changes between cell center and edge. Strength-based events like A3 work poorly, so time or location triggers are used."},
 {id:"e8", t:"Handover",
  p:["1. Phone sends a <b>Measurement Report</b>. 2. Source decides and sends a <b>Handover Request</b> to the target (X2 or Xn, or through the core on S1 or NG). 3. Target admits and replies.",
     "4. Source sends the handover command: <code>RRCConnectionReconfiguration</code> with <code>mobilityControlInfo</code> (LTE) or <code>RRCReconfiguration</code> with <code>reconfigurationWithSync</code> (NR).",
     "5. Phone starts <b>T304</b>, syncs to the target and does random access (usually contention-free).",
     "6. Phone sends Reconfiguration Complete to the target. 7. Target asks the core for a <b>path switch</b>. 8. Source context is released.",
     "<b>Failures:</b> too late (radio link fails before the command arrives), too early, wrong cell, T304 expiry (RACH to target failed).",
     "<b>Conditional handover (CHO):</b> the command is given early and the phone executes it when a condition is met. Useful when the serving link may die suddenly."],
  say:"Report, prepare, command, random access to the target, complete, path switch.",
  trap:"Handover success rate has a preparation part and an execution part. A drop before the command is not counted as a handover failure unless you look for it."},
 {id:"e9", t:"Radio link failure and re-establishment",
  p:["RLF triggers: <b>T310 expiry</b>, <b>random access failure</b> (max preambles), <b>RLC maximum retransmissions</b>, handover failure (T304).",
     "After RLF the phone starts <b>T311</b>, finds a cell, and sends an <b>RRC re-establishment request</b> with a cause (otherFailure, handoverFailure, reconfigurationFailure).",
     "Re-establishment works only if security was active and the target cell has (or can fetch) the phone's context.",
     "If it fails, the phone goes to idle and NAS recovers with a service request or registration. The user sees a longer gap.",
     "RLF reports and re-establishment counters are the raw data behind the 'drop rate' KPI."],
  say:"A failure is declared by T310, RACH, or RLC. Then the phone tries to re-establish, and if that fails it starts over from idle.",
  trap:"A drop is not always a radio problem. An unexpected RRC release from the network also ends the call. Check who ended it."},
 {id:"e10", t:"Idle mode: selection, reselection, paging",
  p:["<b>Cell selection:</b> the cell must pass the S criterion: <code>Srxlev = measured RSRP - (q-RxLevMin + offset) - Pcompensation > 0</code>.",
     "<b>Reselection:</b> by frequency priority first, then by ranking (R criterion) with hysteresis.",
     "<b>PLMN selection:</b> home and preferred PLMNs first. This decides when a phone uses a roaming or satellite PLMN.",
     "<b>Paging:</b> the phone wakes at its paging occasion, once per DRX cycle (for example 1.28 s), and checks for P-RNTI.",
     "A paged phone answers with random access and a Service Request."],
  say:"The phone camps where the S criterion passes, on the highest priority layer, and wakes once per paging cycle.",
  trap:"If <code>q-RxLevMin</code> is set too low, phones camp on a cell they cannot actually reach in the uplink. Camping succeeds, RACH fails."}
]},

{id:"ntn", t:"Satellite RAN and direct-to-cell", s:"What changes when the cell tower moves at 7.7 km/s.", lessons:[
 {id:"f1", t:"The physics: distance, speed, delay, Doppler",
  p:["A low Earth orbit satellite flies at about <b>7.7 km/s</b>, a few hundred kilometers up. SpaceX gives this speed in its own direct-to-cell update.",
     "<b>Delay:</b> light covers 300 km per millisecond. At 550 km straight overhead the one-way delay is about 1.8 ms. At a low elevation the slant range can pass 1,500 km, about 5 ms.",
     "<b>Delay changes fast:</b> as the satellite passes, the path length changes by kilometers per second. The timing advance must follow.",
     "<b>Doppler:</b> shift = (v / c) x f x cos(angle). At 1.9 GHz and 7.7 km/s the worst case is about <b>48 kHz</b>. LTE subcarriers are only 15 kHz apart.",
     "<b>Doppler rate:</b> the shift sweeps from positive to negative during a pass, by hundreds of Hz per second.",
     "<b>Path loss:</b> FSPL(dB) = 32.45 + 20 log10(km) + 20 log10(MHz). At 550 km and 1900 MHz that is about 153 dB. A 5 km terrestrial link at the same frequency is about 112 dB.",
     "<b>Phone limits:</b> 0.2 W transmit power and a low-gain antenna. The satellite needs a very large, sensitive antenna array to close the uplink."],
  say:"Compared with a tower, a satellite link has about 40 dB more path loss, milliseconds of extra and changing delay, and Doppler several times the subcarrier spacing.",
  trap:"Do not say 'latency is the main problem'. For LEO the delay is small. Changing delay, Doppler and link budget are the hard parts."},
 {id:"f2", t:"Two ways to do it: NTN-aware phones or unmodified phones",
  p:["<b>3GPP NTN (Release 17 and later):</b> the phone knows it is talking to a satellite. It has GNSS, reads the satellite orbit from system information, and corrects its own timing and frequency before it transmits.",
     "<b>Direct-to-cell with unmodified LTE phones:</b> the phone thinks it sees a normal tower. All the correction must happen on the network side.",
     "SpaceX states that its service works with unmodified LTE phones, using partner operator LTE spectrum in the 1.6 to 2.7 GHz range, and that the satellite must handle Doppler and timing so that LTE still works.",
     "Network-side tricks, from first principles: pre-shift the downlink frequency and timing so they are correct at the beam center. Then every phone in the beam only sees the small leftover (residual) error.",
     "Smaller beams mean smaller residual Doppler and delay spread across the beam, but more beams and more handovers.",
     "SpaceX has also said that altitude, beam size, elevation angle and satellite count were chosen to sit just at the edge of where LTE works. So margins are thin, and validation has to find the edges.",
     "Industry reports say Starlink plans to add NR-NTN as phone chipsets with Release 17 support become common."],
  say:"With NTN-aware phones the phone compensates. With unmodified phones the satellite must make itself look like a normal cell, and only the residual error is left for the phone.",
  trap:"Details of SpaceX's implementation are not public. Label your reasoning as reasoning. Interviewers respect that more than a confident guess."},
 {id:"f3", t:"Release 17 NR-NTN feature list",
  p:["<b>SIB19:</b> satellite ephemeris, common timing advance parameters, epoch time, validity duration (<code>ntn-UlSyncValidityDuration</code>), <code>cellSpecificKoffset</code>, <code>kMac</code>, stop-serving time, reference location.",
     "<b>Timing advance:</b> the phone computes its own part from GNSS position and ephemeris, and adds the common TA broadcast by the network (for the feeder link).",
     "<b>Frequency:</b> the phone pre-compensates uplink Doppler on the service link.",
     "<b>K_offset:</b> an extra scheduling offset added to uplink timings (PUSCH after a grant, HARQ-ACK, Msg3) so the large timing advance still fits.",
     "<b>HARQ:</b> up to 32 processes, and feedback can be disabled per process. Then reliability comes from repetitions and RLC.",
     "<b>Timers extended:</b> random access response window, contention resolution timer, SR prohibit timer, DRX HARQ timers, RLC reassembly, PDCP discard.",
     "<b>Mobility:</b> earth-fixed, quasi-earth-fixed and earth-moving cells. Conditional handover with time-based and location-based triggers. Neighbor satellite info for measurement timing.",
     "<b>Scope:</b> transparent payload, FR1, bands n255 (L-band) and n256 (S-band). Release 17 also defined IoT-NTN for NB-IoT and LTE-M.",
     "<b>Where to read:</b> TR 38.811 and TR 38.821 (studies), TS 38.300 clause 16.14 (overview), TS 38.331 (RRC, SIB19), TS 38.213 (timing), TS 38.101-5 (UE RF for NTN)."],
  say:"Release 17 gives the phone the satellite's orbit, lets it pre-correct time and frequency, stretches the timing relationships with K_offset, and relaxes HARQ and timers.",
  trap:"Release 17 NTN needs GNSS in the phone. An unmodified legacy phone does none of this, which is why the two approaches are tested differently."},
 {id:"f4", t:"What breaks, layer by layer",
  p:["<b>Sync:</b> uncorrected Doppler puts PSS/SSS outside the phone's search range. The phone never sees the cell.",
     "<b>PHY:</b> residual frequency offset causes inter-carrier interference. Low SINR forces low MCS. Doppler rate stresses the phone's frequency tracking loop.",
     "<b>PRACH:</b> timing uncertainty across a wide beam can exceed what the preamble format tolerates. The base station must search a wider window.",
     "<b>Timing advance:</b> the absolute delay is beyond the normal TA range, and it drifts quickly, so TA commands must be frequent or the timeline must be shifted on the network side.",
     "<b>MAC:</b> the RAR window, contention timer and fixed HARQ timing (n+4 in LTE FDD) all assume a short round trip.",
     "<b>RLC / PDCP:</b> reordering and poll timers set for terrestrial HARQ delay fire too early.",
     "<b>RRC:</b> every phone in a beam must hand over when the satellite moves on, about the same time. Radio link failure timers must tolerate short gaps.",
     "<b>NAS / core:</b> PLMN selection and roaming behavior decide when a phone goes to the satellite at all. Tracking areas must make sense while cells sweep across the ground."],
  say:"I go up the stack: can the phone sync, can it RACH, does timing hold, does HARQ timing fit, do the layer 2 timers fit, and does mobility survive a satellite pass.",
  trap:""},
 {id:"f5", t:"What a validation engineer would test",
  p:["<b>Access:</b> time from no service to registered, RACH success rate versus elevation angle, attach success across phone models.",
     "<b>Link quality:</b> residual frequency offset, timing advance distribution, BLER and MCS versus elevation, uplink power headroom.",
     "<b>Mobility:</b> handover success during beam and satellite changes, drop rate over a full pass, recovery time after a gap.",
     "<b>Services:</b> SMS delivery time and success, data session setup, throughput per beam, voice quality where offered.",
     "<b>Interoperability:</b> many phone models and modem chipsets (Qualcomm, MediaTek, Samsung, Apple), because each tolerates Doppler and timing differently.",
     "<b>Coexistence:</b> the phone should prefer the terrestrial network and return to it quickly. Test the edges of coverage.",
     "<b>Regression:</b> every new eNB/gNB software build runs the same automated suite, and KPIs are compared build over build.",
     "<b>Lab first:</b> a channel emulator adds satellite delay and Doppler profiles so a pass can be replayed on the bench. Then confirm in the field under real satellites."],
  say:"I would test access, link quality, mobility and services across many phone models, first with emulated passes in the lab and then under real satellites.",
  trap:""},
 {id:"f6", t:"Link budget walk-through",
  p:["<b>Received power (dBm)</b> = transmit power + transmit antenna gain - path loss - other losses + receive antenna gain.",
     "<b>Noise floor (dBm)</b> = -174 + 10 log10(bandwidth in Hz) + noise figure.",
     "<b>SNR</b> = received power - noise floor. Compare it with the SNR needed for the lowest MCS.",
     "Uplink example for one RB (180 kHz): phone 23 dBm, phone antenna about 0 dBi, path loss about 160 dB at low elevation. That leaves -137 dBm before satellite antenna gain.",
     "Noise in 180 kHz is -174 + 52.6 = -121.4 dBm, plus the receiver noise figure. So without antenna gain the signal is far below noise.",
     "The satellite antenna must supply the missing tens of dB. SpaceX describes phased arrays of about 2.7 m by 2.3 m for this.",
     "Ways to help the uplink: fewer RBs (same power in less bandwidth), low MCS, repetitions, HARQ combining."],
  say:"I add up powers and gains, subtract path loss, compare with the noise floor, and see how many dB of margin are left.",
  trap:"Giving the phone fewer RBs raises SNR per RB but lowers the data rate. That is the uplink trade-off in one sentence."}
]},

{id:"kpi", t:"KPIs and parameter tuning", s:"How RAN performance is measured and improved.", lessons:[
 {id:"g1", t:"KPI families",
  p:["<b>Accessibility:</b> can users get in? RACH success rate, RRC setup success rate, bearer or PDU session setup success rate, attach or registration success rate.",
     "<b>Retainability:</b> do they stay? Drop rate = abnormal releases / all releases.",
     "<b>Mobility:</b> handover success rate (preparation and execution), ping-pong rate.",
     "<b>Integrity:</b> how good is it? Throughput, latency, BLER, packet loss.",
     "<b>Availability and utilization:</b> cell uptime, RB usage, number of connected users.",
     "A KPI is a ratio of counters. Always ask what the numerator and denominator count and over what period."],
  say:"I group KPIs into accessibility, retainability, mobility and integrity, and I always check how each one is counted.",
  trap:"A 99% success rate over 100 attempts and over 100,000 attempts are very different claims. State the sample size."},
 {id:"g2", t:"Which parameter moves which KPI",
  p:["<code>q-RxLevMin</code>: who may camp. Affects accessibility at the edge.",
     "PRACH: <code>preambleInitialReceivedTargetPower</code>, <code>powerRampingStep</code>, <code>preambleTransMax</code>, <code>prach-ConfigIndex</code>, <code>zeroCorrelationZoneConfig</code>. Affect RACH success, access delay and uplink interference.",
     "Uplink power: <code>p0-NominalPUSCH</code>, <code>p0-NominalPUCCH</code>, <code>alpha</code>. Affect uplink SINR, interference and phone battery.",
     "Failure timers: <code>T310</code>, <code>N310</code>, <code>N311</code>, <code>T311</code>. Affect drop rate versus recovery time.",
     "Handover: A3 offset, hysteresis, time-to-trigger, filter coefficient. Affect handover success and ping-pong.",
     "DRX timers and the inactivity timer: latency and signaling load versus battery.",
     "Link adaptation: BLER target, outer-loop step sizes, CQI report period. Affect throughput.",
     "RLC: <code>maxRetxThreshold</code>, <code>t-PollRetransmit</code>, <code>t-Reordering</code>. Affect drops and latency.",
     "TDD pattern and scheduler weights: downlink versus uplink capacity, fairness versus peak rate."],
  say:"For each KPI I know two or three parameters that move it, and what each one costs somewhere else.",
  trap:"Every tuning change has a cost on another KPI. Always name the trade-off."},
 {id:"g3", t:"A sound tuning method",
  p:["1. Write down the goal KPI and the guard KPIs that must not get worse.",
     "2. Record a baseline under fixed conditions (same build, same phones, same RF setup, same traffic).",
     "3. Change one parameter at a time.",
     "4. Collect enough samples to see past random variation. Repeat runs.",
     "5. Compare distributions, not single numbers: median, 10th and 90th percentile.",
     "6. Check the guard KPIs and check real phones, not only the best one.",
     "7. Confirm in a different condition (other phone model, other signal level, field).",
     "8. Record the change, the evidence and how to roll back. Add a regression test."],
  say:"Baseline, one change, enough samples, compare distributions, check side effects, confirm elsewhere, document.",
  trap:"Do not call something an improvement because one number went up on one setup. Check that the setup matches real use."},
 {id:"g4", t:"From counters to dashboards",
  p:["Sources: base station counters and traces, phone logs, test tool results (iperf, ping), core logs.",
     "Store raw results with keys you will filter on: build, phone model, band, cell config, test case, timestamp.",
     "Typical queries: success rate per build, percentile throughput per phone model, top failure causes, build-over-build change.",
     "Good dashboards show trend, spread and sample count, and link each point back to raw logs.",
     "Alert on change from baseline, not only on fixed thresholds."],
  say:"I store results with the right keys, then compute rates and percentiles per build so regressions show up on their own.",
  trap:""}
]},

{id:"logs", t:"Logs and debugging", s:"Tools, what to read at each layer, and a method that works.", lessons:[
 {id:"h1", t:"The tool map",
  p:["<b>QXDM</b> (Qualcomm): real-time diagnostic logging from Qualcomm modems. <b>QCAT</b> decodes and analyzes the saved logs offline.",
     "<b>XCAL</b> (Accuver): drive-test collection tool. It drives the phones, records modem logs plus GPS, and runs test scripts. <b>XCAP</b> is its post-processing tool.",
     "<b>Shannon DM</b> (Samsung): the same role as QXDM for Samsung Exynos modems.",
     "Other names you may hear: MediaTek ELT, Keysight Nemo, Rohde &amp; Schwarz QualiPoc, Wireshark for S1AP, NGAP and GTP.",
     "<b>Network-side logs</b> (your Keysight protocol logs) show the same messages from the other end, plus what the scheduler decided.",
     "<b>TRACE32</b> is a hardware debugger (JTAG). It is for firmware-level debugging, a deeper level than protocol logs.",
     "The skill that transfers is reading the layers, not the tool's menus."],
  say:"Every modem vendor has its own log tool, but they all show the same things: over-the-air messages, layer 1 and MAC statistics, and events. I read the layers, and match them to the network-side log.",
  trap:"Be exact about which tools you have used. Knowing one vendor tool well transfers. Pretending to know another does not."},
 {id:"h2", t:"What to read in a UE log",
  p:["<b>Serving cell:</b> band, channel number, PCI, RSRP, RSRQ, SINR, over time.",
     "<b>Over-the-air messages:</b> RRC and NAS messages with timestamps. This is the backbone of every timeline.",
     "<b>RACH:</b> attempts, preamble power, RAR received or not, timing advance value, Msg3 and Msg4 result.",
     "<b>Downlink:</b> MCS, number of RBs, rank, CQI reported, BLER, HARQ retransmissions.",
     "<b>Uplink:</b> grants, transmit power, power headroom, BSR, uplink BLER.",
     "<b>Layer 2:</b> RLC retransmissions, status reports, PDCP statistics.",
     "<b>Events:</b> radio link failure, re-establishment, handover start and end, timer expiries.",
     "In QXDM these appear as numbered log packets, for example LTE RRC OTA (0xB0C0) and NR RRC OTA (0xB821). Check exact IDs before you quote them."],
  say:"I build a timeline from the RRC and NAS messages, then lay the radio measurements and MAC statistics next to it.",
  trap:""},
 {id:"h3", t:"A debugging method you can say out loud",
  p:["1. <b>State the symptom exactly:</b> what failed, how often, since which build, on which phones.",
     "2. <b>Reproduce</b> it and find the smallest setup that still fails.",
     "3. <b>Build a timeline:</b> align phone log, network log and test script log on one clock.",
     "4. <b>Find the last good message</b> and the first wrong or missing one.",
     "5. <b>Pick the layer:</b> if a message was sent but not received, go down. If it was received and rejected, go up.",
     "6. <b>Compare</b> with a passing run: different build, different phone, different config.",
     "7. <b>Make one hypothesis and test it</b> by changing one thing.",
     "8. <b>Classify:</b> phone, base station, core, configuration, RF setup, or the test itself.",
     "9. <b>Report:</b> evidence, root cause or best narrowing, and a regression test."],
  say:"Symptom, reproduce, timeline, last good message, pick the layer, compare with a pass, test one hypothesis, report with evidence.",
  trap:"Jumping to a guess before building the timeline is the most common mistake under interview pressure. Say 'first I would build the timeline'."},
 {id:"h4", t:"Network-side view",
  p:["When the base station is the product, you also read its own logs: MAC scheduler traces, PHY measurements (uplink SINR, timing offset, frequency offset), RRC and S1AP/NGAP traces, counters.",
     "The base station sees what the phone cannot: uplink quality, why the scheduler did not grant, whether a preamble was detected, what the core answered.",
     "The phone sees what the base station cannot: downlink quality, whether it decoded the DCI, its own power and timers.",
     "Root cause often needs both views. A message 'sent' on one side and 'never received' on the other localizes the fault to the air link or the layers below.",
     "Core side: S1AP/NGAP messages such as Initial UE Message, Initial Context Setup, UE Context Release with cause."],
  say:"The two logs are two halves of one story. Where they disagree is usually where the bug is.",
  trap:""}
]},

{id:"test", t:"Test strategy and automation", s:"What to test, how to plan it, how to automate it so it can be trusted.", lessons:[
 {id:"i1", t:"Test types, with RAN examples",
  p:["<b>Functional:</b> does the feature do what the spec says? Example: handover completes with the right messages.",
     "<b>Performance:</b> how well? Example: throughput, attach time, handover interruption time.",
     "<b>Interoperability (IOT):</b> does it work with every phone and chipset? Each modem vendor reads the spec a little differently.",
     "<b>Regression:</b> did the new build break something that worked?",
     "<b>Stress, soak, stability:</b> many users, long duration, repeated cycles. Finds leaks, slow degradation, rare races.",
     "<b>Negative:</b> wrong inputs, lost messages, timer expiries, rejects.",
     "<b>Conformance:</b> formal 3GPP test specs (TS 36.521 and 36.523 for LTE, TS 38.521 and 38.523 for NR) used for certification (GCF, PTCRB)."],
  say:"Functional proves it works, performance proves how well, interoperability proves it works with everyone, regression proves it still works.",
  trap:""},
 {id:"i2", t:"What a test plan contains",
  p:["Scope: the feature, what is in and out.",
     "References: the requirement and the 3GPP clause it comes from.",
     "Test environment: build, phones, RF setup, core, tools.",
     "Test cases: ID, purpose, preconditions, configuration, steps, expected result, pass/fail criteria, KPIs to record.",
     "Coverage matrix: bands, bandwidths, phone models, signal conditions, mobility cases.",
     "Entry and exit criteria, priorities, risks, schedule.",
     "Results tracking: where results go, how failures are triaged, who signs off."],
  say:"A test plan ties each test to a requirement, defines pass and fail before the run, and says how results are tracked to sign-off.",
  trap:"Pass/fail criteria written after the run are not criteria. Define them first."},
 {id:"i3", t:"Framework design",
  p:["<b>Layers:</b> equipment drivers at the bottom, a test library in the middle, test cases on top, an orchestrator beside them, a results database and reports at the end.",
     "<b>Drivers:</b> instruments over SCPI on LAN, programmable attenuators, phones over ADB or AT commands, power over PDU, consoles over UART.",
     "<b>Reliability:</b> timeouts on every wait, bounded retries, state recovery, health checks before and after, clean teardown even on failure.",
     "<b>Evidence:</b> on failure, automatically save phone logs, network logs and the config used.",
     "<b>pytest tools:</b> fixtures with scopes for setup and teardown, <code>parametrize</code> for the coverage matrix, markers to select suites, <code>conftest.py</code> for shared fixtures, xdist for parallel runs, hooks for custom reports.",
     "<b>Parallelism:</b> one worker per device or per RF chain. Shared equipment needs a lock."],
  say:"Drivers, library, tests, orchestrator, results. Every wait has a timeout, every failure saves evidence, every test cleans up after itself.",
  trap:""},
 {id:"i4", t:"Flaky tests in an RF lab",
  p:["Common causes: RF leakage, loose connectors, shared equipment, phone left in a bad state by the previous test, timing races in the script, network state not reset (forbidden lists, back-off timers).",
     "Decide first: is it the test, the setup, or the product? A real intermittent product bug looks the same as a flaky test.",
     "Tools: rerun in isolation, rerun on another device, compare logs of pass and fail, add health checks, add state reset.",
     "For radio behavior, a statistical pass rule is honest: for example at least 98 of 100 attach attempts succeed.",
     "Quarantine a flaky test with a ticket and an owner. Do not delete it and do not let it block everything."],
  say:"I treat an intermittent failure as real until I can show it is the test. Then I fix the test, not hide it.",
  trap:"Blind retries hide real intermittent bugs. Retry only what you can explain, and log every retry."},
 {id:"i5", t:"CI/CD and build-over-build comparison",
  p:["Every build: a short smoke suite (attach, data, detach).",
     "Nightly: the full regression suite across the phone matrix.",
     "Weekly or pre-release: soak and stress.",
     "KPI gates: block a release if a key KPI drops more than an agreed amount against the baseline.",
     "When a regression appears, bisect the builds to find the first bad one.",
     "Keep test code in Git, reviewed, versioned with the product build it targets."],
  say:"Smoke on every build, full regression nightly, KPI gates before release, and bisect when a number moves.",
  trap:""},
 {id:"i6", t:"When the base station is the device under test",
  p:["Your experience: phone or modem is the DUT, a network emulator is the known-good partner.",
     "This job: eNB/gNB software is the DUT, phones are the known-good partner. The messages are the same. The pass/fail question moves to the other side.",
     "New test tools you would meet: real phones in shield boxes, UE simulators that act as many phones at once, channel emulators that add fading, delay and Doppler, and a core network (real or simulated).",
     "New kinds of tests: capacity with many users, scheduler fairness, admission control, counters and KPIs correctness, behavior with misbehaving phones.",
     "What stays the same: the framework, device control, log capture, triage, dashboards, CI."],
  say:"It is the same conversation tested from the other side. My automation and debugging carry over, and the base station internals are what I need to learn fastest.",
  trap:"Do not say 'it is the same thing'. Say what is the same and what is new."}
]},

{id:"lab", t:"Lab and RF", s:"dB math, components, and a troubleshooting checklist.", lessons:[
 {id:"j1", t:"dB math in your head",
  p:["dB is a ratio. <b>+3 dB</b> is double, <b>+10 dB</b> is ten times, -3 dB is half.",
     "dBm is power relative to 1 mW. 0 dBm = 1 mW, 10 dBm = 10 mW, 20 dBm = 100 mW, 23 dBm = 200 mW, 30 dBm = 1 W.",
     "dBi is antenna gain relative to an isotropic antenna.",
     "Thermal noise is <b>-174 dBm/Hz</b>. Noise floor = -174 + 10 log10(bandwidth) + noise figure.",
     "Example: 4.5 MHz (a 5 MHz LTE carrier) gives -174 + 66.5 = -107.5 dBm. With a 7 dB noise figure the floor is about -100.5 dBm.",
     "RSRP is per resource element. Full-carrier power is higher by 10 log10(12 x number of RBs). For 25 RBs that is about 24.8 dB."],
  say:"Three dB doubles, ten dB is ten times, and noise is minus 174 dBm per hertz before bandwidth and noise figure.",
  trap:"Do not add dBm to dBm. Add dB to dBm."},
 {id:"j2", t:"Lab components",
  p:["<b>Attenuators</b> (fixed and programmable): set path loss and simulate distance or fading.",
     "<b>Splitters and combiners:</b> a 2-way splitter loses a bit over 3 dB per branch.",
     "<b>Shield boxes:</b> isolate a phone from live networks and from other tests.",
     "<b>Cables and connectors</b> (SMA, N): every one adds loss and can fail. Use the right torque.",
     "<b>Circulators, duplexers, filters:</b> separate transmit and receive paths and bands.",
     "<b>Channel emulators:</b> add fading, delay and Doppler in a repeatable way.",
     "<b>Instruments:</b> spectrum analyzer (what is on the air), signal generator, power meter, vector network analyzer (cable and component loss).",
     "<b>Reference clock:</b> instruments share a 10 MHz reference so their frequencies agree."],
  say:"Attenuators set the link, shield boxes keep it clean, and a spectrum analyzer tells me what is really on the cable.",
  trap:""},
 {id:"j3", t:"Conducted and over-the-air testing",
  p:["<b>Conducted:</b> cable from the instrument to the phone's antenna port. Repeatable, but it skips the antenna.",
     "<b>Over the air (OTA):</b> through antennas, in a shield box or chamber. Includes the antenna, less repeatable.",
     "<b>Calibrate path loss</b> for each path and frequency, and store it. Without it, RSRP numbers mean nothing.",
     "<b>Leakage:</b> a phone that secretly sees a live network, or a neighbor test bench, gives results that look like random bugs.",
     "<b>Level planning:</b> too little attenuation overloads the receiver. Too much hides the signal in noise."],
  say:"Conducted for repeatability, OTA when the antenna matters, and calibration before I trust any level.",
  trap:""},
 {id:"j4", t:"RF troubleshooting checklist",
  p:["1. Is the cell actually on? Check the instrument state and look at the spectrum.",
     "2. Right band, channel number and bandwidth on both sides?",
     "3. Connectors tight, right ports, no damaged cable? Swap the cable.",
     "4. Is the level in range? Check for overload and for too much loss.",
     "5. Is the path loss calibration current for this frequency?",
     "6. Any leakage in or out? Check with the cell switched off: does the phone still see something?",
     "7. Reference clock locked?",
     "8. For MIMO: are both antenna ports connected to the right paths?",
     "9. Is the phone in the right mode (band lock, airplane mode off, correct SIM and profile)?",
     "10. Change one thing, retest, write it down."],
  say:"I check that the signal exists, that both sides agree on frequency, that the path and levels are right, and that nothing leaks.",
  trap:""},
 {id:"j5", t:"Keeping a lab reliable",
  p:["Remote power control (PDU) and serial consoles so a hung device can be recovered without walking to the lab.",
     "Health checks before every run: device responds, cell is up, path loss as expected.",
     "Monitoring and alerts for hosts, devices and services.",
     "Equipment booking or locking so two tests do not fight over one instrument.",
     "Inventory of what is connected where, kept up to date.",
     "A clear owner per bench and a simple way to report a broken setup."],
  say:"A reliable lab is remote-recoverable, monitored, and never shares equipment silently.",
  trap:""}
]}
];

